00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.554
Anyways, they have written
this great blog post

00:03:12.554 --> 00:03:13.970
because he was
working with trying

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.726
to call C functions from
BigQuery with WebAssembly.

00:03:16.726 --> 00:03:18.350
And he was doing this
because he wanted

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:22.310
to make the data accessible for
others to be able to explore.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.260
And they have terabytes
of data, and they

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.220
thought it would be
very cool if their data

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.380
sets could be made available
for anyone to easily analyze.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:32.160
And so he's got this blog
post, explains how he does it.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.330
And what's fun is that
he connected with one

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.420
