00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:39.750
MARK MANDEL: Very much so.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.300
MELANIE WARRICK: --and
they wrote up a blog post

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.335
as well to explain how
to make it go faster,

00:03:43.335 --> 00:03:45.210
and especially when
you're running C and Rust

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.400
code at scale.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.890
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, no, he
does these really cute tricks

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.710
with using [INAUDIBLE]
to basically embed C,

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.840
and then [? Felipe ?] also
does Rust code as well

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.970
to basically make it
run on top of BigQuery

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:57.350
using its JavaScript engine.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.570
It's kind of neat, actually.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:00.822
I'm kind of impressed with it.

00:04:00.822 --> 00:04:02.030
MELANIE WARRICK: That's cool.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.285
And anything else, Mark?

00:04:03.285 --> 00:04:05.632
Because I think you might
want to talk about Kubernetes,

00:04:05.632 --> 00:04:06.215
is that right?

00:04:06.215 --> 00:04:06.890
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:08.931
I haven't spoken about
Kubernetes for the last 30

00:04:08.931 --> 00:04:10.470
seconds, so I should
bring that up.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.540
Another fantastic teammate
of ours, Sandeep Dinesh,

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.550
has started a series around
Kubernetes best practices.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.339
Kicking it off, he's talking
about how and why to build

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:21.320
small container images.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.600
So he has a blog post that's
up on the Google Cloud

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.660
Blog talking about how to use
Alpine to build small images,

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.630
as well as using multi-step
docker build processes to also

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:30.680
help with that pipeline.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.170
To go along with that, there's
also a series of YouTube videos

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:34.100
as well that you can watch.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.110
And we'll link to all
that in the show notes.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.630
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes,
and they're also linked

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:38.760
at the bottom of the blog.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:39.200
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.190
MELANIE WARRICK: All right.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.300
Well, Mark, I think it's
time to go talk with Eric.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.841
MARK MANDEL: Let's
go chat with Eric.

00:04:43.841 --> 00:04:44.660
Today I am--

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.910
I guess the term would
possibly be a little bit giddy.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.500
I'm sitting in a
small, confined room

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.050
here with Eric Brewer, VP of
Infrastructure here at Google.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.620
Eric, how are you doing today?

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.500
ERIC BREWER: I am doing great.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.670
Happy to be in the little room.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:56.420
MARK MANDEL: Happy
to be in the room?

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:56.940
ERIC BREWER: It's awesome!

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:57.343
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:04:57.343 --> 00:04:58.150
MELANIE WARRICK: Definitely.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.780
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, thanks
for coming to join us.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.946
I know we want to talk about
some open source stuff,

00:05:01.946 --> 00:05:03.240
some distributed system stuff.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.430
But before we get
stuck into that,

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.900
can you tell us a little
bit about who you are,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.250
what do you do here at Google,
a little bit of your background?

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.000
ERIC BREWER: Sure.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.230
I am, prior to Google,
best known as faculty

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:14.090
at UC Berkeley in
computer science.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.430
And out of that work,
I founded a company

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.270
called Inktomi, which did
search engines before Google,

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.860
actually, and pioneered
a lot of the modern ways

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.460
that we actually build what
you now call cloud computing.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.334
So for example, Inktomi
was the first company

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:30.730
to use large-scale
clusters in the style

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:34.130
that Google does now, and
pioneered that as a technology

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.142
for building
large-scale systems.

00:05:36.142 --> 00:05:38.600
And then I've done a bunch of
work in developing countries.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.450
And then for the last six
years, I've been VP at Google,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:45.140
working on this second revisit
of all of the problems of cloud

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:45.759
computing.

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:47.300
MELANIE WARRICK: So
what does it mean

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.614
to be VP of Infrastructure?

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:50.030
ERIC BREWER: It's
a good question,

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.674
because I believe I'm the only
VP that has no direct reports.

00:05:53.674 --> 00:05:55.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.640
MELANIE WARRICK:
How does that work?

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.806
MARK MANDEL: So basically,
you do whatever you want.

00:05:58.806 --> 00:06:02.180
ERIC BREWER: I am really a
senior architect for computing

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.430
for Google, both the cloud
part and the internal part,

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:10.400
and I view my role as to help
with the strategy of Google

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.500
Cloud, and also the tactics.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:15.560
But in particular, what
is our tactical design

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:18.165
for how we want to build,
really, the next cloud?

00:06:18.165 --> 00:06:20.310
And what we want
Cloud to be, versus

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.267
what it's been historically.

00:06:22.267 --> 00:06:24.600
MARK MANDEL: And I believe
congratulations are in order.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.957
You just won an award.

00:06:25.957 --> 00:06:27.540
ERIC BREWER: I was,
yesterday, elected

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.120
to the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.010
which is founded by--

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.150
Thomas Jefferson and others
were the inaugural members.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:39.510
And it's, I would say,
the US's elite association

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:42.620
for scientists and
artists, and also includes

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.060
business people, including
Larry and [? Sergey ?]

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.570
and several other Googlers, and
many people from Berkeley who

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:49.140
I know.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:50.640
MELANIE WARRICK: And I
believe you also mentioned,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.890
before we started the
podcast, that Barack Obama is

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:53.670
part of this?

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:56.287
ERIC BREWER: He is joining this
year as well, as a member, so.

00:06:56.287 --> 00:06:58.620
MELANIE WARRICK: And we were
also talking about the fact

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.120
that you've actually met him.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.980
ERIC BREWER: I have
met him a couple times.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.390
MELANIE WARRICK: And
he offered you a job.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:04.890
ERIC BREWER: Last
time I met him was

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.110
in October, about
three years ago,

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.880
when he did offer me a job.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.532
And his point was he had
a year and a half to go,

00:07:13.532 --> 00:07:15.990
maybe a little more, and he
felt the main way he could have

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.330
impact in that time-- because
Congress was a little bit

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:19.410
divisive--

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.550
was to work on agencies and
have the agencies make progress.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:26.970
And he felt, correctly, that
they needed technical help.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.970
And he recruited several people
from the Bay Area to do that.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.541
I, in the end, declined--

00:07:32.541 --> 00:07:35.040
in part because I like what I'm
doing at Google, but in part

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.300
because I have kids.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.400
It was not a good
time to move to D.C.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.590
But if I could serve
some other time,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:41.700
I'd certainly consider it.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.825
And I've also worked with
Bill Clinton in the past,

00:07:43.825 --> 00:07:46.140
and so I'm familiar with
the general impact you can

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:47.580
have that way, which is great.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.980
But I wasn't willing
to move this time.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.675
MELANIE WARRICK: And you've
helped create USA.gov, too.

00:07:52.675 --> 00:07:55.050
ERIC BREWER: That was the
project I did with Bill Clinton

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.250
in the year 2000.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.880
And that was the first
federal search engine,

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.670
and covered all of the
US government properties

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:03.280
at that time.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.940
That was a fun project for sure,
and he was a very interesting

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:06.930
guy to work with.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.870
And then that expanded more,
in the past-- without my help--

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:12.300
to cover all the states
and include other things

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.959
like commerce and ID systems,
authentication for citizens

00:08:15.959 --> 00:08:17.250
to get the government services.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.290
It's quite a broad thing
now, but originally it

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.094
was really just a
fancy search engine

00:08:21.094 --> 00:08:22.260
for all the federal content.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:22.600
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:23.280
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:24.420
Well, speaking of history--

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.270
I didn't realize this, and I
feel a little ashamed that I

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:28.240
didn't-- you have a long
history with Kubernetes.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.990
In fact, you were there
straight from the beginning.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:32.429
Can you tell us a little bit
about your involvement there?

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:33.240
ERIC BREWER: Sure.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:37.380
It started as a small project
with a few different people--

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:40.409
Brendan Burns, Joe
Beda, Craig McLuckie,

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:43.110
and some people that are
[INAUDIBLE] associated with it

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.930
now-- think people like
Brian Grant, Tim Hawkin.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.630
My role was to help them
form it into something

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.140
that made sense for Google--

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.410
from a project--
and honestly, to

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:56.640
do extensive internal sales
to get Google on board

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.980
that Kubernetes was a
bet we needed to take,

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:00.570
and that it needed
to be open source.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.649
And both of those were
controversial at the time.

00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:04.690
MARK MANDEL: And so I'm
really curious, as well--

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:06.330
why, back then, did
you feel like this

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:07.800
was such an important project?

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.200
I mean, obviously, it
seems to have done OK now.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.540
But in retrospect, I'd love
to see your perspective.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.550
ERIC BREWER: I think
the reasoning was sound,

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.584
and I think the track
record proves that.

00:09:16.584 --> 00:09:18.250
But the reasoning had
a couple of parts.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:21.540
The first part is that the
cloud, especially then but even

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.390
now, is, in my mind,
way too focused

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:27.630
about the infrastructure--
the machines, the disks.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.990
And that is not the
path to productivity.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.590
The path to
productivity is thinking

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:35.040
about services and
APIs and how you

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.372
build things that
are highly available,

00:09:37.372 --> 00:09:39.330
and you don't want to
worry about the machines.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:41.040
You really don't even want
to know the machines exist,

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:41.770
frankly.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:43.311
It'd be nice if we
could just pretend

00:09:43.311 --> 00:09:45.690
they don't exist at all,
and Kubernetes is absolutely

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:47.370
a step in that direction.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.830
So that was part of it,
and I would say my mission

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.500
at Google is to get the new
cloud to be something that's

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.750
about services and
APIs that has nothing

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:55.990
to do with infrastructure.

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:56.520
It's there.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:57.360
We will manage it.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.650
We'll make it secure.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:01.170
But as a developer, you
shouldn't need to know.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.480
The second aspect
is that it needed

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.130
to be open source, because
it's too big a shift for Google

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:08.965
to do by itself.

00:10:08.965 --> 00:10:11.220
If we're going to change
the definition to cloud,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.670
it has to be done
broadly, and open source

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:16.940
is the best way
to get that done.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.230
And I think there's a
third aspect, which is--

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:23.820
if we make the playing field
about services and containers,

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.260
Google has a relative advantage.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.530
If the playing field is
about virtual machines,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:32.135
we can do that, but we started
very late in that game.

00:10:32.135 --> 00:10:34.260
Whereas if you look at
containers, that's something

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.000
we've been doing for
a decade in Borg.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.160
We have vastly more
experience on how

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.879
to do that than anyone else.

00:10:39.879 --> 00:10:41.670
We literally invented
a bunch of the pieces

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.610
in Linux to make that
possible and understand them

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.940
more deeply than anyone else.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.440
So we should be betting
on the places we can win,

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:49.804
and this is a place we can win.

00:10:49.804 --> 00:10:51.720
MELANIE WARRICK: You
also had mentioned to us,

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:55.080
before we started, that you gave
the first talk on Kubernetes

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:59.130
ERIC BREWER: I gave the launch
talk at DockerCon in 2014.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.080
We released Kubernetes that day.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.480
I gave one of the two keynotes.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.090
Solomon Hykes of Docker
gave the other one,

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.880
where he introduced
Swarm, which for a while

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:10.299
competed with Kubernetes.

00:11:10.299 --> 00:11:11.715
But we had the
advantage that when

00:11:11.715 --> 00:11:13.465
we talked about
Kubernetes, it was already

00:11:13.465 --> 00:11:15.990
working to some degree, and
people could play with it.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.710
And that really helped us
get off to a good start.

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.900
And frankly, it's well
designed, and it actually works.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.200
And so that was a big
factor in it being

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.160
able to really take this space.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.080
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:30.500
And since then, our ecosystem,
especially at Google Cloud,

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.350
of open source tooling
has really grown.

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:33.210
We've got Istio.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.000
We've got OpenCensus.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.082
How do you see that
ecosystem going forward?

00:11:37.082 --> 00:11:39.290
ERIC BREWER: I think it's
a core part of our strategy

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:40.460
to be the Open Cloud.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:45.430
That was the talk I gave
at Next in 2016 or '17.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.270
I can't remember which one.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:48.440
I guess 2017.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:53.900
But part of it is that we want
to make workloads more mobile.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:56.870
That is to our advantage if
you use open source things that

00:11:56.870 --> 00:12:00.260
can run on-prem, in other
clouds, and on Google.

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:04.190
Because now we're competing
on operations, on cost,

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:05.210
on technology.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:07.130
And that's the battle
we want to compete in.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:07.629
Right?

00:12:07.629 --> 00:12:10.015
So open source inevitably
allows a couple

00:12:10.015 --> 00:12:11.390
of things that
are helpful to us.

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.840
But the most important one in
my mind, besides community,

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:18.920
is that you can move workloads
more easily to GCP over time.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:19.670
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.950
And I know you're particularly
interested in Istio.

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.040
Can you explain where that
fits into the whole strategy

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:27.130
of where you're seeing
that workloads go?

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.420
ERIC BREWER: Istio has many
things I like about it,

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:32.540
but I think the way to think
about it is, really, it's

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:33.920
once you get a
little bit bigger,

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.360
you want to decouple the
operations of your clusters

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:41.900
from the developers who are
developing new services.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:44.480
And the reason Istio does
this is, essentially,

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:48.260
that it's intercepting traffic
on its way into a service

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:50.840
so that you can automate
the telemetry you

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:51.830
get on every service.

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:56.690
You can automate policies
about access control and DDoS.

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:59.090
And so all the control things
you want to think about

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:02.210
for a service, you put in
the Istio layer and not,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:04.610
in particular, in
the source code.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:05.960
So we call this externalizing.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.060
I'm saying, take
all the policy stuff

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:09.890
that you currently
have hidden somewhere

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:12.400
in the code of your micro
service, take it out.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:16.080
Externalize it so that we can
manage those things from Istio.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.620
And that has two big wins.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.200
First is, it makes it consistent
for all your services,

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.690
because now you don't have
to update all your services

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.810
every time you want to change
a policy, which is intractable.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.690
But also, as a
developer, it turns out

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:32.150
it makes you have more
freedom in the sense

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:35.540
that you don't have to
negotiate with operations

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:37.700
what the policy should be.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:38.200
Right?

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.190
You worry about what
your service does.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:42.620
Operations worries about
what the policies are.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:44.180
And we remove this
friction that's

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:45.710
inherent if you have to--

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.660
on every call site--
actually agree

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:49.101
on what it is you're doing.

00:13:49.101 --> 00:13:49.600
Right?

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.629
So we're taking out
coordination overhead,

00:13:51.629 --> 00:13:54.170
which I don't think people will
appreciate till they have it.

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:56.640
But having been on both sides,
they will appreciate it.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:56.960
MARK MANDEL: Hmm.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:59.360
MELANIE WARRICK: In terms of the
next cloud, as you were saying,

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:00.818
what are you excited
about in terms

00:14:00.818 --> 00:14:03.480
of what would be built off of
that once we get to a place

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.804
we're able to abstract more
of the actual back end?

00:14:05.804 --> 00:14:07.220
ERIC BREWER: Well,
I actually feel

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:08.886
like people using
Kubernetes are already

00:14:08.886 --> 00:14:12.320
getting a lot of this value
now in that if you're building

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.520
an application out of
a collection services,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.030
and I mean, a good
example, Spotify.

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:19.980
Spotify was doing this model
before Kubernetes existed.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:20.480
Right?

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.105
They had already kind
of bought into we

00:14:22.105 --> 00:14:23.450
want to have APIs and services.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.116
And they had built some in-house
stuff that was Kubernetes-like,

00:14:26.116 --> 00:14:27.510
actually, to their credit.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.140
But now we can give them
a much stronger platform

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:31.966
to do what they
always wanted to do.

00:14:31.966 --> 00:14:33.590
And when you add
Istio, what that means

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:35.900
is that our operations
people can actually

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:38.780
manage a collection of
services versus managing

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:39.960
a collection of nodes.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:40.460
Right?

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:41.997
So that even the
language they use

00:14:41.997 --> 00:14:43.580
to talk about what
it is they've built

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.240
and how they're managing it
has been upleveled to something

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:49.310
that saves them a
lot of time and toil.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:50.000
MARK MANDEL: There was a quote.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:51.580
I'm going to attribute
it to Kelsey Hightower.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.760
I think it was him that,
talking about Kubernetes,

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:55.970
is you deal with the
application layer and not

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.050
the infrastructure layer.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:58.060
Does that sort of
resonate for you?

00:14:58.060 --> 00:14:58.768
ERIC BREWER: Yes.

00:14:58.768 --> 00:15:00.662
That is absolutely--
when I say new cloud,

00:15:00.662 --> 00:15:02.120
I really mean you
should worry only

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:04.310
about the applications
and services

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:05.930
that you're trying to build.

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:06.500
MELANIE WARRICK:
What are the biggest

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:08.270
limitations that we have that--

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:09.364
barriers to the new cloud?

00:15:09.364 --> 00:15:11.030
ERIC BREWER: Many
things, I would think.

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:13.550
First of all, there's a
whole bunch of legacy stuff

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:17.420
that is really about which
OS version do I need,

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.370
and how big do my
nodes have to be,

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:21.620
and how do I do
security patches?

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.930
Those are problems that
you shouldn't have to have,

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:24.490
I would say.

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:26.720
But there's a lot of
legacy tooling around

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:29.580
how you manage the actual nodes
and OS images you're using.

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:30.080
Right?

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:32.706
I would like that to just
disappear in some sense.

00:15:32.706 --> 00:15:34.580
If you use GKE, it does
disappear, because we

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:35.990
manage the OS for you.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:37.725
We do the security
patches for you.

00:15:37.725 --> 00:15:39.350
But there's still a
lot of legacy stuff

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:41.071
that-- it works that
way, and tooling

00:15:41.071 --> 00:15:42.070
and all kinds of things.

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:44.120
So that's part of the challenge.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:48.110
I would say another challenge is
even the core pieces like Linux

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:50.870
itself, it kind of
expects you to deal

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:52.680
with devices for networking.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:53.180
Right?

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.096
Other parts of the system,
like a file system,

00:15:55.096 --> 00:15:57.110
is kind of easy to abstract.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:16:00.020
You can make it look like
part of a process model.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.150
The network and
devices, more generally,

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:04.190
are still very tied to the node.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:08.210
So we're kind of
hindered by our legacy

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:10.580
use of nodes in various places.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.700
But networking in devices is the
one that stands out most to me

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:15.270
at the moment.

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:17.290
And that's not easy to
fix, because changing

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:20.050
that requires changing a
whole bunch of open source

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:24.040
software that's using the
device-level interfaces,

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.590
IP tables, things like
that, that are inherently

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:27.940
part of the node.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:30.070
And we will change
those things over time.

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:31.510
But that's not a quick process.

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:33.176
MARK MANDEL: Question
that comes to mind

00:16:33.176 --> 00:16:35.410
is we talk a lot about
hybrid cloud, at Google Cloud

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:36.520
especially, especially
when we're talking

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:37.870
about things like Kubernetes.

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:40.330
How do you see
that new cloud-type

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:42.040
of strategy, or, like,
forward thinking,

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.990
working into hybrid
cloud-type scenarios?

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:47.860
ERIC BREWER: Well, we do see a
lot of people using Kubernetes

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:49.000
on-prem already.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.860
And that is because
it's open source,

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:53.560
it was very easy for people
to try it, and play with it,

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:54.900
and use it themselves.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:56.710
So a side effect
of that is we've

00:16:56.710 --> 00:17:01.030
actually been building
allies inside companies

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:02.920
who are using
Kubernetes that will

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:05.560
want to move those workloads
to cloud over time.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:07.234
And I am pretty
sure they'll find

00:17:07.234 --> 00:17:09.400
that the best place to move
your Kubernetes workload

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:10.021
will be GCP.

00:17:10.021 --> 00:17:10.520
All right?

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:13.150
GKE is still
significantly better

00:17:13.150 --> 00:17:15.730
than any other
deployment of Kubernetes.

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.562
And it's managed for you.

00:17:17.562 --> 00:17:19.770
And so if you just want to
focus on your application,

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:23.211
it's the best manifestation
of that vision so far.

00:17:23.211 --> 00:17:24.460
MARK MANDEL: Is hybrid cloud--

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:25.640
I don't know if I want
to use this right term--

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.300
but I'll put it this way.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:30.430
Do you think hybrid cloud
is an almost stepping stone

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:31.990
in the future of
cloud technologies,

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:33.615
like we have to go
through hybrid cloud

00:17:33.615 --> 00:17:34.900
to get to maybe a full cloud?

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:37.150
Or do you think that's
probably just going to continue

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:38.869
to evolve, in terms of like--

00:17:38.869 --> 00:17:39.910
ERIC BREWER: I think we--

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:40.150
MARK MANDEL: [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:42.108
ERIC BREWER: --have to
go through hybrid cloud.

00:17:42.108 --> 00:17:43.990
And I think it's
not a quick process.

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:47.650
Moving to the cloud for a
large enterprise isn't--

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.135
not going to be a
single-day event.

00:17:49.135 --> 00:17:49.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:50.701
ERIC BREWER: Right?

00:17:50.701 --> 00:17:52.392
It's going to be
a 10-year event.

00:17:52.392 --> 00:17:54.850
And they'll move some workloads,
and later other workloads,

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:57.340
and they will use
multiple clouds, honestly.

00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:58.440
That's fine.

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.840
But what it means is if you
want to preferentially get

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.240
future workloads to
move to your cloud,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:07.090
you're better off interacting
with those workloads now.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:07.590
Right?

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.580
So what I think about
it is every time

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:11.980
there's a Kubernetes
cluster on-prem

00:18:11.980 --> 00:18:15.040
and we can help it
in a variety of ways,

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:18.790
that has a net present value to
us of being a future customer

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:22.870
load that's more
likely than average

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:24.700
to be on Google in the future.

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:26.500
We can disproportionately
win more

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:28.189
than our share of
those workloads.

00:18:28.189 --> 00:18:30.730
MELANIE WARRICK: And I'm going
to play a little bit of a role

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:32.260
that Mark likes to
play on my podcast--

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:32.759
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:32.759 --> 00:18:34.360
MELANIE WARRICK: --that I drive.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.910
What exactly is hybrid
cloud, for those

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:38.970
who may not know the term?

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:41.050
ERIC BREWER: It has a
few interpretations,

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:42.760
in part because
different vendors want

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:43.926
it to mean different things.

00:18:43.926 --> 00:18:48.040
So it's always going to be a
little bit confused as a term.

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.980
But what we mean
by it is that you

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:55.540
can have some of your workloads
on-prem, some in our cloud,

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:56.620
some in another cloud.

00:18:56.620 --> 00:19:00.710
And they start to feel like
one thing and not three things.

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:02.740
So an advantage to using
Kubernetes and Istio is

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:05.740
we can make these three
different environments feel

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:07.930
much more similar to each other,

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:11.050
And so for example, if you to
have a policy about which users

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:12.670
can access which
services, you can

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:14.500
enforce that policy
on our cloud,

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:17.560
on another cloud, and
on-prem in a consistent way.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.120
That's what makes
it a hybrid cloud,

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.070
is the consistency
of policy that you

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:23.290
can expect to get over time.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:26.530
Now, it does not always mean I'm
going to take a single workload

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:28.480
and split it across sites.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:29.900
Sometimes that'll happen.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:31.720
It's more common that you'll
partition your workloads,

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:33.719
and some will run here,
and some will run there,

00:19:33.719 --> 00:19:35.860
and that you'll be in
the progress of migrating

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:39.120
some of them pretty much all
the time for the next 10 years.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:39.740
Right?

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:43.690
It's a constant state
of evolving things

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:46.210
in a stepwise, safe way.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.460
The other advantage hybrid
has, from my perspective,

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:50.350
is that there's a
variety of reasons

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:53.170
that certain workloads
can't move to cloud today.

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:57.520
Could be regulatory, it could be
certain concerns about locality

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:00.280
of data or latency,
or you don't have

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:03.219
a data center in the right place
for me, or we have sunk cost

00:20:03.219 --> 00:20:05.260
and we need to use our
data centers for five more

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:07.550
years, all kinds of reasons.

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:13.210
And so there's an appetite to
modernize applications on-prem

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:16.180
using Kubernetes today
versus waiting five years,

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:17.961
go to cloud, and then modernize.

00:20:17.961 --> 00:20:18.460
Right?

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.170
So part of the
hybrid strategy is

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.750
to decouple the
movement of cloud

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:24.850
from the modernization
of your apps.

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:27.600
You can now do them in any
order, and we'll help you.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:29.500
And in fact, when
you have a portfolio,

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.440
you'll do a mix of
modernization and movement.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:34.930
And you can control that
mix however you like.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:36.680
MELANIE WARRICK: --as
modular as possible.

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:37.480
ERIC BREWER: Yes.

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:38.950
MELANIE WARRICK: You were
talking before about the value

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:40.040
of open source.

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:42.250
Do you think that there's
any specific things that

00:20:42.250 --> 00:20:46.450
need to be done to help foster
a more comprehensive open source

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:48.250
community out there?

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.200
Anything specific around
the open source space

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:52.480
that you are very
passionate about

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:55.450
or find exciting about
where it's going?

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:57.290
ERIC BREWER: It's quite
a complicated space.

00:20:57.290 --> 00:21:00.820
I would say we learn, despite
having done open source

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:03.760
for a decade, we're
still learning exactly

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:05.890
all of the best ways to
interact with open source.

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:09.160
But I will say Kubernetes, in
particular, has the strongest

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:13.070
community I've ever seen,
in terms of the rate of CLs,

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:17.180
the relative agreement on
kind of general direction,

00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:20.750
and the extensibility, which
means that a lot of arguments

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.760
are avoided, because two
different players can

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:24.600
do the same thing
two different ways

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:25.850
and they're both compatible.

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:28.436
So the structure of Kubernetes,
both the technical structure,

00:21:28.436 --> 00:21:29.810
but also the
community structure,

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:31.400
has been very effective.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:34.040
And it's really a model, I
would say, for other communities

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:35.270
to try to follow.

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:38.190
And it's different, frankly,
than other open source efforts,

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:39.360
even within Google.

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:42.320
So for example, open source
can mean we release the source,

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:44.060
but we control it.

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:46.010
That's kind of
the Android model.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:48.110
It can mean we
take pull requests,

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:50.700
but we have to
moderate them all.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:52.380
That's kind of the
TensorFlow model.

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.320
And so it's still
fairly Googlecentric,

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:57.910
but open to submissions
of improvements.

00:21:57.910 --> 00:21:59.990
Kubernetes model is much
more open than that.

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:03.500
It's really-- the community
is actually moderating

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:05.420
the important changes.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:08.780
And we gave up the trademark,
meaning that the foundation

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:10.350
owns the trademark, not Google.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.980
And the consequence of that,
which is very intentional,

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:16.730
is that we can't retract
the use of the trademark

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:18.410
just because you are
competing with us,

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:20.530
or just because we don't
like the way you're doing it.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:21.030
Right?

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:22.850
So as an enterprise,
it kind of means

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:25.460
that you know Kubernetes
and your right

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:29.130
to use it is not revocable
at Google's whim.

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:30.230
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:32.450
On a slightly different
track, is there

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:35.349
anything you're excited to learn
about in the next year or two?

00:22:35.349 --> 00:22:36.890
Anything in particular
that you would

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:39.830
love to explore, especially
if you had the time for it?

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:40.580
ERIC BREWER: Sure.

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:43.110
I kind feel like I'm
consistently behind on machine

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:43.610
learning.

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.200
And it's not that I
don't know quite a bit

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:46.158
about machine learning.

00:22:46.158 --> 00:22:47.180
I actually do.

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:49.910
It's another space that's
moving very quickly.

00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:51.860
And I don't see how
anyone could keep up

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:54.710
with both Kubernetes
and machine learning

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:56.080
and do it have authentically.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:57.871
MELANIE WARRICK: Agree
with you completely.

00:22:57.871 --> 00:22:59.050
That's why we're both here.

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:00.290
ERIC BREWER: So I--

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:02.400
I have some homework
to do on that front.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.792
And I have some
actual test projects

00:23:04.792 --> 00:23:06.000
I'd like to do at some point.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:07.602
But I'm not getting to them.

00:23:07.602 --> 00:23:08.810
So we'll see where that goes.

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:09.650
MELANIE WARRICK: We'll
see when that happens.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:11.120
By the time you
probably get to them,

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:12.620
something will have
been built to be

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:14.786
able to just automate it
for you, or take care of it

00:23:14.786 --> 00:23:16.190
for you at that point.

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:18.148
ERIC BREWER: I can say
one funny thing, though,

00:23:18.148 --> 00:23:20.610
which is that when I
learned about AI in school,

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:22.950
it was a kind of
a mess of a topic,

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.290
including expert systems.

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:27.080
But one of the topics was
neural nets, which then went out

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:28.925
of favor for quite a long time.

00:23:28.925 --> 00:23:31.550
But it's one of the parts I know
better than others, because it

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:34.140
was still in favor when I
was taught the material.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:35.030
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
that's a good one

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:36.470
to know the way things
have been going lately.

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:37.600
ERIC BREWER: Turned
out to be pretty good.

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:39.590
Now that actually turned
out not to be that helpful,

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:41.840
because the field has
advanced so far that knowing

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.810
the basics of it is not as
relevant as one might expect,

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:46.564
but it's still kind of useful.

00:23:46.564 --> 00:23:47.730
MELANIE WARRICK: Definitely.

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:48.813
MARK MANDEL: That's great.

00:23:48.813 --> 00:23:50.480
So I'm wondering--
looking forward,

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.521
and I'm guessing there's
obviously a lot of stuff

00:23:52.521 --> 00:23:55.529
you can't talk about, but
are there particular areas,

00:23:55.529 --> 00:23:58.070
either open source or at Google
that you think people should,

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.320
say, keep an eye on,
or that you think

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.840
are going to rapidly evolve in
a really positive direction?

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.840
ERIC BREWER: Well, certainly
the short-term ones

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:07.560
are there's a ton going
on in the hybrid space,

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:10.880
and particularly about how
do you create collections

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.220
of clusters that span
different environments

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:16.130
and that you can actually
manage as a single thing.

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.560
And again, that's something
that has to be done--

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.750
Google can lead it, but it
can't be done by Google alone.

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:24.800
It's a big project that
fundamentally is going

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:27.240
to involve a lot of partners.

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.680
And we'll see a lot of progress
on that this year, for sure.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:33.200
MELANIE WARRICK: So we
know KubeCon is coming up.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:36.166
And this is the big
Kubernetes conference.

00:24:36.166 --> 00:24:37.790
Can you tell us a
little bit about that

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:39.060
and your involvement?

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.990
ERIC BREWER: This is KubeCon in
Europe in May, in particular.

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:44.180
And I've been to
many KubeCons, but I

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:47.300
haven't been to one in Europe
yet, so that'll be good.

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:50.410
Each continent has its own
kind of flavor of KubeCon,

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:53.465
so it'll be good to see what's
different about this one.

00:24:53.465 --> 00:24:55.340
And that is one of the
reasons I'm attending.

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:57.080
There's a bunch of
things I care about.

00:24:57.080 --> 00:24:59.150
So certainly the hybrid
stuff I care about.

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:01.040
I gave a talk in the
last KubeCon on that,

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:02.840
and there's more
coming on that front.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:06.620
But also just, there's a ton
of stuff on machine learning

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:10.689
and Kubernetes, on aspects
of networking and management.

00:25:10.689 --> 00:25:12.980
A lot more users are now in
production with Kubernetes,

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:14.480
so we'll hear some
of their stories.

00:25:14.480 --> 00:25:16.610
So especially in Europe,
that'll be great.

00:25:16.610 --> 00:25:18.920
But also honestly,
it's, again, hard

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:20.240
to keep up with this space.

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.580
And going to KubeCon
just forces me

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:25.760
to spend three days
deep in it and make

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.010
sure I'm caught up again.

00:25:27.010 --> 00:25:28.830
MELANIE WARRICK: See what's
new and what's coming.

00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:30.680
MARK MANDEL: What's been your
most exciting thing that you've

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.810
seen somebody either
extend or build

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:36.271
upon Kubernetes that you've
been like, that's really cool?

00:25:36.271 --> 00:25:37.770
ERIC BREWER: That's
a good question.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:38.150
Yeah.

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:39.899
I think-- one thing I
think is interesting

00:25:39.899 --> 00:25:43.400
is people using clusters
that are disconnected

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:45.590
from the rest of the
internet as a way

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:48.380
to do a highly available
distributed system.

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:50.930
So if you want to run
something highly available,

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:53.810
and you are worried about
network connectivity,

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:55.280
having a local
Kubernetes cluster

00:25:55.280 --> 00:25:57.170
turns out to be an
excellent way to do it.

00:25:57.170 --> 00:25:58.764
And I've seen a--

00:25:58.764 --> 00:26:00.680
I'm not sure which
customers I can talk about,

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:05.360
but you can kind of guess that
if you have an oil platform,

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:08.540
or you have a retail store
that's in a remote location,

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:10.700
you might want to have
some local computing that

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:15.002
can keep your store online
even if your network is down.

00:26:15.002 --> 00:26:16.460
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
that's super cool.

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:18.052
I never actually
thought of that.

00:26:18.052 --> 00:26:18.760
That's very cool.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:19.740
MELANIE WARRICK: Definitely.

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:20.740
ERIC BREWER: And
Kubernetes, because it's

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:22.300
made to support
high availability,

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:23.971
is a very good fit for that.

00:26:23.971 --> 00:26:26.470
MARK MANDEL: So that sounds
like a really great customer use

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:28.690
case for someone who
is using Kubernetes.

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:30.690
I know I do a lot of stuff
extending Kubernetes.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:32.890
I think that the extension
mechanisms are really exciting.

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:34.931
Have you seen any particular
extension mechanisms

00:26:34.931 --> 00:26:37.379
or the extension of
Kubernetes as a platform

00:26:37.379 --> 00:26:39.170
for distributed systems
that you've thought

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:41.050
has been particularly inspiring?

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:42.990
ERIC BREWER: Well,
assuming you mean

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:47.250
CRDs as the extension
mechanism, which is this API

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:50.650
to have your own resources
with their own calls, that

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:52.030
is a fantastic mechanism.

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:54.250
And it's really, I
think, one of the secrets

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:56.140
to Kubernetes' success.

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:57.110
And it's a subtle one.

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:58.840
Because it's not something
you even necessarily learn

00:26:58.840 --> 00:27:01.210
about the first few times
you play with Kubernetes.

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:03.580
But once you want to do
something with it that's

00:27:03.580 --> 00:27:06.000
not what it does,
it comes into play.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:07.750
And the real reason
it's an important part

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:11.770
is, again, it means it's much
easier for different players

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:14.110
to make different
changes and have

00:27:14.110 --> 00:27:17.052
them feel Kubernetes is native
and have them work together.

00:27:17.052 --> 00:27:18.760
MARK MANDEL: So for
those who don't know,

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:20.410
CRD, Custom Resource Definition.

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:22.076
ERIC BREWER: Custom
Resource Definition.

00:27:22.076 --> 00:27:23.830
It's like you make
your own little nouns

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.500
and verbs that extend
the Kubernetes model.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:29.650
But if you do it this way, then
all of the kind of Kubernetes

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:31.960
tooling, including the
Command Line Interface,

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:34.434
actually just support
your new extension.

00:27:34.434 --> 00:27:36.100
And that makes it
very easy to integrate

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:37.017
into existing systems.

00:27:37.017 --> 00:27:39.100
MARK MANDEL: Are there are
any particular examples

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:40.360
of that that come to mind?

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:42.068
ERIC BREWER: We're
using it, for example,

00:27:42.068 --> 00:27:44.590
the way we want to
manage multiple clusters

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:46.085
is just an extension of that.

00:27:46.085 --> 00:27:47.710
That's important
because, again, you're

00:27:47.710 --> 00:27:49.780
going to have clusters on-prem
and in different clouds,

00:27:49.780 --> 00:27:51.190
you'll need to
actually make sure

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:52.570
that you can manage them all.

00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:54.850
I feel like anyone
that wants to do kind

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:57.670
of a functions-as-a-service
platform is going to want

00:27:57.670 --> 00:27:59.230
to use that kind of extension.

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:01.780
Anyone that wants to do
a domain-specific version

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:04.300
of Kubernetes where you kind
of narrow the scope, let's say,

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:07.060
maybe it's for machine
learning, or maybe it's for--

00:28:07.060 --> 00:28:09.730
I don't know-- for retail,
that actually ends up

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:12.350
being a way you can do that.

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:13.810
But I think the
main thing about it

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:16.030
is that because it
integrates with the tooling,

00:28:16.030 --> 00:28:19.540
and because you can have many
of these things co-loaded,

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:23.200
it's very flexible and
avoids a lot of conflict.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:25.780
MELANIE WARRICK: So in terms
of your career and the work

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:28.000
that you've done, who have
been some of the mentors,

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:30.640
or the people that you've
been inspired by, as you've

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:31.745
been going down your path?

00:28:31.745 --> 00:28:32.453
ERIC BREWER: Mmm.

00:28:32.453 --> 00:28:33.453
That's a great question.

00:28:33.453 --> 00:28:34.700
And there's always a bunch.

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:37.130
And you never quite know
who it's going to be,

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:38.770
but let's see.

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:40.740
On the technical
side, I would say

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:43.030
three people that stand
out, technically, Butler

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:45.490
Lampson, who is
now at Microsoft,

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:48.760
but a famous computer scientist
and Turing Award winner who

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:51.100
was on my PhD committee at MIT.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:53.860
Barbara Liskov, who was
my first adviser at MIT

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:57.020
and later won the Turing Award,
and one of the great women

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:00.040
in computer science, and
introduced a bunch of things

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:03.010
that we take for granted,
like abstraction,

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:07.150
the idea that you might want to
have an abstract notion of what

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:09.180
something does behind an API.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.660
That's so obvious now, but
someone had to do that, right?

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:13.561
It doesn't come from nothing.

00:29:13.561 --> 00:29:16.060
And in fact, I think she got
the Turing Award a little later

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:20.350
than she should have because
it was too obvious, which

00:29:20.350 --> 00:29:21.680
is a totally unfair judgment.

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:22.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:23.763
ERIC BREWER: And
actually, this year's

00:29:23.763 --> 00:29:26.410
award winner, Dave Patterson,
who, one of the first faculty I

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:28.020
worked with in
detail at Berkeley.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:31.600
I got to Berkeley in 1994,
worked on the NOW project,

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:34.544
which is about to have its 20th
anniversary, because we measure

00:29:34.544 --> 00:29:36.460
the anniversaries from
the end of the project.

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:39.250
That's the project that
led to cluster computing

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:41.260
for the internet and
Inktomi, and was,

00:29:41.260 --> 00:29:43.810
in some ways, the reason
I won the Sciences

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:46.810
Award, for that train of
work on cluster computing

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:48.694
and large-scale services.

00:29:48.694 --> 00:29:50.110
And so that case,
it's not so much

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:53.146
that Dave was the
technical influence--

00:29:53.146 --> 00:29:54.520
although he's
great technically--

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:56.980
it's that he is a
great leader and showed

00:29:56.980 --> 00:30:00.140
how to do a group project
versus an individual project.

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:02.200
So the NOW project had
four faculty involved.

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:05.860
I was, in some sense, the
fourth, the junior guy.

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:07.450
And they were very
welcoming to me

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:10.060
and also just taught
me a lot about how

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:12.400
is it you do research, how
do you engage students,

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:14.422
how you get things
done that have impact,

00:30:14.422 --> 00:30:15.880
and even what your
goals should be.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:16.420
Right?

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:17.610
The goal should be impact.

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:20.082
And I've followed
that advice forever.

00:30:20.082 --> 00:30:21.040
MELANIE WARRICK: Agree.

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:24.370
Any resources that you'd
recommend for anything that you

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:27.550
particularly find that you're
a fan of around the Kubernetes

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:30.250
space, around containers,
around distributed computing?

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:32.333
ERIC BREWER: Well, you
mentioned Kelsey Hightower.

00:30:32.333 --> 00:30:34.270
So anything he says
is worth watching,

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:36.860
and I enjoy the
time I get with him.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:40.150
And he's a nice complement
to me in the sense

00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:43.620
that I have some academic
background he doesn't have--

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:44.940
not that he's not super sharp.

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:45.640
He is.

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:48.760
But he has a tremendous sense
of the on-the-ground reality

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:49.840
for Kubernetes.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:52.270
And he can keep me honest
when I'm being a little too

00:30:52.270 --> 00:30:52.970
ivory towerish.

00:30:52.970 --> 00:30:53.720
MARK MANDEL: Right

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:54.636
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:30:54.636 --> 00:30:57.310
ERIC BREWER: And so it's a
great relationship for me,

00:30:57.310 --> 00:30:58.930
but also I feel
like it's something

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:00.280
everyone can benefit from.

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:03.310
And you often can make
Kubernetes, which is, frankly,

00:31:03.310 --> 00:31:05.890
overly complicated-- we can
talk about that if you like--

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:09.070
he can show you the simple ways
to get to what you want to do.

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:10.029
And that is a true art.

00:31:10.029 --> 00:31:11.403
MARK MANDEL:
Unfortunately, we're

00:31:11.403 --> 00:31:12.577
heading up on the half hour.

00:31:12.577 --> 00:31:15.160
But before we wrap up, is there
anything we haven't mentioned,

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:17.160
or anything you want to make
sure that is on the podcast

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:19.590
that our listeners hear about
before we finish off here?

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:22.380
ERIC BREWER: I guess I would say
that you should think about all

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:25.530
the toil that you have
in the job you might have

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:28.060
as a developer
and how much of it

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:30.394
we should be able to get rid
of over the next few years.

00:31:30.394 --> 00:31:30.893
Right?

00:31:30.893 --> 00:31:32.490
Because part of the
move to Kubernetes

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:34.140
is to reduce the toil.

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:36.330
And the simple things,
we can already do,

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:38.610
like you're less
aware of your node,

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:40.710
and we can patch the OS for you.

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.140
But we should be able to
automate a bunch of things,

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:46.050
like we're looking at automating
the testing process much more.

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.850
And maybe we can
tell, you know, you're

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:49.584
running a bad
version of open SSL.

00:31:49.584 --> 00:31:51.000
You might want to
get rid of that.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:51.500
Right?

00:31:51.500 --> 00:31:52.950
That's something
we can scan for,

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:54.569
and we should be
able to tell you.

00:31:54.569 --> 00:31:56.610
And maybe, if you'll let
us, we can automatically

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:57.730
patch that too.

00:31:57.730 --> 00:31:58.230
All right?

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:00.060
So there's a degree
of automation

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:02.730
that's possible when you get to
this higher-level abstraction

00:32:02.730 --> 00:32:04.740
that's not possible
if all you have

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:06.720
is the OS image in the nodes.

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:08.820
And that-- and by
the way, that also

00:32:08.820 --> 00:32:11.970
enables a huge new
use of AI, which

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:15.687
is have the AI tell
us what changes you

00:32:15.687 --> 00:32:16.770
should make to your thing.

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:18.480
Because we actually
now have data,

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:21.970
and we are talking at a
higher-level abstraction,

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:25.112
which helps reasoning of all
kinds, human and artificial.

00:32:25.112 --> 00:32:26.070
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:26.700
MELANIE WARRICK: I agree.

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:28.920
That'll be a wonderful thing to
be able to have more automation

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:30.330
to help with that, as well.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:31.109
Thank you again.

00:32:31.109 --> 00:32:33.150
We really appreciate you
coming on to the podcast

00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:35.280
and sharing all things
Kubernetes, containers,

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:37.739
distributed systems, and
hearing about your mentors, too.

00:32:37.739 --> 00:32:38.530
That was wonderful.

00:32:38.530 --> 00:32:39.060
Thank you.

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:39.850
ERIC BREWER: My pleasure.

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:40.900
MARK MANDEL: Thank you so much.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:42.000
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
Eric, thank you again

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:43.570
for joining us on the podcast.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:45.970
It was wonderful to hear
about open source distributed

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:46.722
systems.

00:32:46.722 --> 00:32:48.930
Well, actually what I think
I enjoyed most about that

00:32:48.930 --> 00:32:50.320
was hearing about
his mentors, too.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:51.180
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that was cool, too.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:52.096
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:32:52.096 --> 00:32:54.540
Anyways, so Mark, if I want
to visualize the network

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:58.020
traffic between my pods
within a Kubernetes cluster,

00:32:58.020 --> 00:32:59.700
is there an easy way to do this?

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:01.020
MARK MANDEL: There is
an easy way to do this.

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:01.860
MELANIE WARRICK: What?

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:02.460
MARK MANDEL: I have--

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:04.085
I've actually tweeted
about this a lot.

00:33:04.085 --> 00:33:06.570
This is a secret presentation
weapon that I have.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:07.070
Yeah.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:08.030
So don't tell anybody.

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:08.416
[EERIE MUSIC]

00:33:08.416 --> 00:33:08.802
SPEAKER 1: Shhh.

00:33:08.802 --> 00:33:09.190
SPEAKER 2: Shhh.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:09.856
SPEAKER 3: Yeah.

00:33:09.856 --> 00:33:12.070
This is top secret
stuff here, all right.

00:33:12.070 --> 00:33:13.410
Totally classified.

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:14.260
Don't tell anyone.

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:14.807
Not a soul.

00:33:14.807 --> 00:33:15.890
[INTERPOSING VOICES] Shhh.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:17.310
MARK MANDEL: Be very,
very quiet about it.

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:18.900
There is a great
open source project

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:20.941
from a company called
Weaveworks, where they have

00:33:20.941 --> 00:33:22.110
a thing called Weave Scope.

00:33:22.110 --> 00:33:24.480
And it's super pretty,
if nothing else.

00:33:24.480 --> 00:33:29.160
Basically, you can install it on
top of your Kubernetes cluster,

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:30.850
or they have a managed
platform as well.

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:32.683
But what it gives you,
which is really nice,

00:33:32.683 --> 00:33:35.720
is a really beautiful,
topographical visualization

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:37.720
of exactly what's happening
inside your cluster.

00:33:37.720 --> 00:33:39.176
So it's seeing
pods, and services,

00:33:39.176 --> 00:33:40.300
and all that kind of stuff.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:42.060
But it also introspects
your network traffic,

00:33:42.060 --> 00:33:43.440
so you can actually
see which systems

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:44.970
are talking to which
systems, which systems

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:46.410
are talking to the
public internet,

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:47.330
that kind of cool stuff.

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:48.840
And it just gives you
a really great way

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:51.173
to visualize exactly what's
happening inside your system

00:33:51.173 --> 00:33:52.500
and whom is talking to whom.

00:33:52.500 --> 00:33:53.550
MELANIE WARRICK: I like
it when I can actually

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:55.890
see what's going on, like
show you some visuals on that.

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:56.640
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:56.640 --> 00:33:59.550
So usually-- I use that a
lot when doing presentations,

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:01.210
because it's really pretty.

00:34:01.210 --> 00:34:02.580
And then it gives you a
really nice visualization

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:03.371
of what's going on.

00:34:03.371 --> 00:34:04.580
And you zoom in and out.

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:05.760
And it's pretty neat.

00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.950
MELANIE WARRICK: I'm
enjoying saying Weaveworks.

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:08.219
MARK MANDEL: Weaveworks.

00:34:08.219 --> 00:34:08.719
Yes.

00:34:08.719 --> 00:34:10.380
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's a good name.

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:10.934
All right.

